A||||15||theogre||Why some places won't let you pump gas.||||5||
Z||000000||theogre||07-27-2001||11:56 PM||||In another thread, <BR>Fast Richard mentioned he has trouble with getting his car filled by station attendants. "We live in one of those progressive states that does not allow self serve gas…"<P>maryjane asked... "Just wonderin, what is the thinking behind not being able to pump your own gas? We're mature enough to pilot several thousand lbs of automobile down the road at 70+ mph, but they're afraid we might spill a gallon of gas?"<P>I’ve started this into another thread because I expect there is a bunch of people that would like to know about this. Several states have bans on self-serve fueling. There are a number of reasons behind it. Some are political but most aren't. I used to live in NJ (another state with this ban) years ago so I've been following this awhile. I also worked in a gas station with mixed pumps. Half SS half we pump.<P>The simple fact is many people can't manage this without spilling gas. (That includes some pump jockeys....) It's not a small amount of people either. The most common spill is a pint to a quart but when you figure a bunch of ninnies dump that every day in just one station you've got a big problem. Figure in a single area there can be dozens to hundreds of stations.... You're talking hundreds to thousand of gallons a week in waste on the ground.<P>Raw fuel is a major component in Smog. This is why you've got vapor sucker nozzles everywhere now.<P>The reason fuel pumps have concrete parking around them is because fuel eats asphalt. It soaks into concrete and eventually works out the bottom. There is it free to work into the soil. Soil contamination is a big issue in the fuel pumping business.<P>It's also a fire hazard. You would not believe the number of complete retards that won't shut off the car or worse smoke while fueling. There are also concerns now that certain cell phone designs may also ignite fuel vapor. <P>In big self serve stations it is all too common to drive up on fairly large puddles. The gas you leave behind could kill the person who is unfortunate enough to drive over the puddle.<P>Yet another fire problem is people filling gas cans on insulated surfaces like truck bed liner. A number of fires have been started by static electric discharge during filling of gas cans.<P>While lead was eliminated from fuel about 1987, we now have compounds like MTBE fouling the ground water, rivers, and lakes. MTBE has proven “difficult” to filter from water, as in the water you drink. Aside from imparting a nasty taste, the health issues around such compounds are not fully known. MTBE is or will be banned in a number of states. CA is one I believe has banned it already. In its place the fed’s require oxygenates like Ethanol that is bloody expensive. CA figures Ethanol will raise the price of gas considerably. Unlike some of the lighter components of gasoline, MTBE doesn’t evaporate. It is picked from the concrete by rain and carried in the runoff to nearby soil streams and rivers.<P>With the increasing federal pressure to limit air pollution, you aren’t likely to see these bans lifted. If anything many cities and some states may have to enact them to meet clean air rules.<BR><P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||5
Z||000001||danny boy||07-28-2001||12:09 AM||||I work for SUNOCO, and we use Ethanol now. And yes, the number of stupid people in this country is alarming. At the plant we have a Pacific pride fueling center. At least once a day there is a big spill. We keep a whole lot of kitty litter on hand...<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fast Richard||07-28-2001||12:26 AM||||I posted this before in another thread before theogre started this one, but it fits better here so once again...<P>This may veering a bit away from Fieros, but to answer MaryJane's question... every couple years in this state, two sides square off on the self-serve issue. So far the no-pumpers have prevailed. The bottom line is, what is to be gained by going to self-serve? The pro self-serve folks claim it will save money, speed up the gas station experience, and remove one more "evil" government intervention from their lives. They point to other states (many of them) that have self-serve with no apparent problems. The no-pumpers point out the true fact that in many self-serve states, prices are higher than they are here. They fear the loss of jobs at the stations (albeit low-paying), and grumble about having to pump their own gas and getting their hands and Armani suits soiled by gasoline. Personally, I don't give a rat's nose one way or the other. To make this argument even more ridiculous is the "card lock stations" here, where certain folks such as police, public service vehicles, and businesses can pump their own fuel for "business purposes". Kind of honks me off to drive by and see some smartly dressed couple gassing up the BMW at 10 pm for "business purposes". End of rant.<BR>BTW....good tip about telling the attendant to set the nozzle on low speed to top off the Fiero.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||JayUSA77||07-28-2001||12:36 AM||||i just wanted to say i live in california and yes the gas prices are enormous and mtbe is banned in all station now.  But i dont get one thing 90% of the people who spill that much gas also *****  about how much it cost and what not... Some people are just plain air headed.huh||||reg||1
Z||000004||DecadenceR||07-28-2001||02:41 AM||||I did some research on this a few years ago for a school report, and I talked with a few people and found out a few interesting things.<P>-In NJ (i don't know anything about the other states) if a person is seen pumping their own gas, the station can be find $5000 per person per instance. The inspector also has the right to shut down the station immediately if it's a recurring problem.<P>-The gas station is supposed to alert the EPA if any more than 2 tablespoons of gas is spilled. (although we all know this rarely happens, and they have a habbit of spilling it all over the sides of our cars)<P>And if anyone ever notices, when us NJ people go out of state and fuel up, the attendant always knows we're from NJ. We're the only people that sit at the pump for 5 minutes before we realize we need to get out lazy rears out of the car and do it ourselves...||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||07-28-2001||03:14 AM||||I live in CA, near San Francisco infact.  Gas is expensive here (and everywhere) because the pricefixers run a pump-price-vs-purchase volume curve, find the peak for every geography, and set the prices accordingly.<P>Extortion. Not because of adding Ethonal.<P>I hope to god we begin to burn 100% ethonal soon, this dinosaur bisuness is silly in the extreme :)<P>I'd just assume pump my own gas in principal, in practice the pumps are all nasty and I carry around disinfectant whipes in my cars because of it.  And yes, people are dumb.  But I've yet to see a lake of gasolene <BR>(thank god, I'd probally be toast, we know the Fiero really uses an external combustion engine, at least the 4-cyls, heh.) Dosen't mean it doesn't happen, and it doesn't mean that gallons of lquid flashes off into the atmosphere every day.  <P>Heck, you can SMELL you're at a gas station some places.<P>We can idoit proof the pumps, the cars, the Spork you are legally required to eat with, but we cannot idoit proof the idoits...<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000006||Monkeyman||07-28-2001||05:53 AM||||I manage a convenience store/gas station in central Indiana.  Any clay based cat litter will suck up most gasoline spills if caught in time.  Why not save a few bucks and hire one guy to keep a look out for spills and clean them up instead of 5 guys to pump your fuel?  Gas spillage is definitely a problem (I see it [and clean it up] every day), but if it's taken care of immediately, there shouldn't be any problems.  Like I said, you throw some clay based cat litter on the spill, wait a few minutes, and scoop it up into a plastic trash bag.  How much easier can it be?<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||07-28-2001||01:10 PM||||MM, you're one of the few that watches and cleans up the mess. Most don't. Even at small stations. <P>At the station I worked I had 3 SS and 3 FS pumps. I was often their by myself. There is no way I can fully watch the SS pumps beyond taking the cash if I'm busy with an FS customer. The odds were good that when the SS people spilled fuel I wouldn't be able to get to it.<P>To make this worse, the majority of SS fuel stations in many areas are mainly conveinence stores with fuel pumps. They pay beween min wage and $8 an hour and small ones have 1-3 employees per shift. They are too busy to keep checking the pumps even if they cared to do so. Since most SS stations are either credit card reader pumps or Pay Inside for cash, there is no reason for these people to go outside.<P>In NJ on of the big things used to promote changes to SS fuel is to keep the station people inside. Why? So they won't get shot at night by some crackhead. (It has happened.) Do you really think the one attendant locked in his cage is going out to clean up spills? No. He's going to stay in there most of the night. <P>Yes, I do support SS fuel in NJ between 11 and 6-7 the next morning. Specifically for attendant safety. The problem is once they get 11-7 inside then they'll fight for 24 hour. This way one guy can "watch" a batch of pumps. <P>We have stations exactly like that in DE now. Merrit are all that way. One attendant to 12 or more pumps. The only time they come out of the cage is when shift changes. <P>It's not any better at the "super size" WAWA stations around here. These stores barely have enough people to cover inside sales of food and stuff. They only go outside to empty trash bins and fill glass wash buckets.<P>These huge stores are increaseing in frequency. I have at least 3 within 10 miles. Such stations often have 20-30 cars filling at one time. No-one watches and No-one cleans up the spills.<P>Cleaning up the spill with "cat litter" solves the fire problem at the pump but unless the material used to clean up the spill is put into sealed containers, the fuel still ends up evaporating into the air.<P>Then what... even the sealed material ends up in compactor trash haulers. If it's not torn open in them it very likely will be when the huge machines at the land fill run over it.<P>barring large spills by total idiots.... We'll just figure what ends up on the side of cars and small (1-2 sqare feet) puddles on the ground. These aren't huge amounts... <P>Figure each small station in an area dumps 1/2 to one gallon of fuel a day total. Figure a big station dumps at least 2. Now add up that there are dozens to hundreds of stations in many areas. It's allot more fuel than most people think.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Black88GT||07-28-2001||03:13 PM||||Maryland is self serve, I've never driven or seen any puddles of fuel.  I actually rarely see people spill more then a few drops.  Maybe its certain areas I dunno but it seems OK here.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||07-28-2001||03:54 PM||||Was in Oregon, earlier this summer. Didn't realize it was FS only. The first time I got out of the car and headed toward the pump, the attendant got REAL excited. <BR>It *did* get good to me, though. Until then, I haven't had anybody offer to wash my windshield for about 10-15 years. Was actually kind of nice.<BR>We asked a few people, during the course of the week, and most folks seem to think that the full-serve laws were there to promote employment. The prices seemed to be very close to Washington's, which is self-serve. <BR>Both states were about 30-40 cents/gallon more than GA, though.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000010||maryjane||07-28-2001||04:25 PM||||Thanks to theogre and others for the explanations of ss vs fs gas. Gas spillage here in Texas is a problem as is ground water contamination. Myself and my family hold a fair amt of Exxon-Mobil stock, but I am not happy with "big oil's" treatment of polution problems. I also know that temp shortages, such as was blamed for this spring's high prices, is a bunch of bull. According to the qtrly & yearly report I get,Exxon is the largest holder of the worlds proven oil reserves and has replinished what it has refined for most of the last decade, so there should be no such thing of a temporary shortage.<BR>Sorry I got off topic and began to rant.||||reg||1
